# A/B: removing three of the four per-frame copies of the block-decision set
date: 2026-08-13
host: mac (Apple aarch64, 12 logical cores)
base: 841c583bf   cand: 29847e5d3 (perf: the block-decision set was carried in FOUR copies)
harness: rust/tools/perf_ab.sh — interleaved paired rounds, randomised per-round
         order, one untimed warmup inside each sample, NEITHER SIDE NICED.
build:   both arms `RUSTFLAGS=-C llvm-args=-align-all-functions=4` (the
         code-placement-lottery control from rav1d-safe docs/AGENT_BRIEF.md §2;
         without it a t=1 delta on a large-working-set cell is a draw from a
         ~1.4% placement lottery). No `-C target-cpu=native` on either arm.
grid:    content=gradient sizes=[256 512 1024] presets=[6 10] qp=40 rounds=15
load:    box idle at start (top foreign process 5.1% CPU, load1 1.96); waited
         140 s for a foreign `mem_probe_encode` (99% of one core) to finish.
ident:   every cell ident=Y (candidate .obu == base .obu). Separately, the full
         168-cell `tools/byteid_fingerprint.sh` grid is unchanged AND equal to
         the C reference.

## Identity control (base vs a byte-identical COPY of the same binary)

This is the noise floor. It is NOT a code-placement control — both arms are the
same file — so it bounds harness + thermal noise only; the alignment flag above
is what handles placement.

| size | preset | n  | ratio  | [p25, p75]       |
|-----:|:------:|---:|-------:|:-----------------|
|  256 | 6      | 15 | 1.0046 | [0.9905, 1.0099] |
|  256 | 10     | 15 | 1.0061 | [0.9795, 1.0151] |
|  512 | 6      | 15 | 1.0012 | [0.9953, 1.0047] |
|  512 | 10     | 15 | 1.0052 | [0.9959, 1.0132] |
| 1024 | 6      | 15 | 1.0025 | [0.9977, 1.0062] |
| 1024 | 10     | 15 | 1.0141 | [0.9843, 1.0190] |

Widest control band: 1024/p10 at [0.9843, 1.0190]. Read anything inside
[0.984, 1.019] as NULL.

## Candidate

| size | preset | n  | base ms  | cand ms  | ratio  | speedup | [p25, p75]       |
|-----:|:------:|---:|---------:|---------:|-------:|--------:|:-----------------|
|  256 | 6      | 15 |  13.3300 |  13.0440 | 0.9789 |  1.022x | [0.9723, 0.9850] |
|  256 | 10     | 15 |   1.8948 |   1.7675 | 0.9328 |  1.072x | [0.9238, 0.9443] |
|  512 | 6      | 15 |  41.7152 |  40.9205 | 0.9867 |  1.014x | [0.9767, 0.9892] |
|  512 | 10     | 15 |   8.4868 |   7.9385 | 0.9323 |  1.073x | [0.9238, 0.9392] |
| 1024 | 6      | 15 | 141.5025 | 137.1844 | 0.9702 |  1.031x | [0.9610, 0.9763] |
| 1024 | 10     | 15 |  30.8675 |  27.6172 | 0.8998 |  1.111x | [0.8776, 0.9037] |

Every p10 cell clears the control band by 4-9 percentage points; the p6 cells
clear it by 1-4. p6 gains less because its per-block decision work is a smaller
fraction of a much larger per-block RD search.

## Profile that motivated it (pre-change)

`/usr/bin/sample`, 1 ms, 15 s steady state, gradient 512x512 preset 10,
11,486 self samples, leaf (self-time) attribution:

| binary                       | share of self time |
|------------------------------|-------------------:|
| perf_encode (the port)       |             75.53% |
| libsystem_malloc.dylib       |             11.85% |
| libsystem_platform.dylib     |             10.91% |
| libsystem_kernel.dylib       |              1.72% |

The same measurement on the C reference (`benchmarks/`-adjacent
`sample_C_p10_512.txt`, 2026-08-07 round, 15,470 samples): malloc 0.49%,
platform 2.70%. So the port's alloc+libc-mem excess over C is ~19.6 points of
its own self time, i.e. an arithmetic ceiling of 1/(1-0.196) = **1.24x**, not
the 1.33x that quoting the port's 22.8% alone would suggest.

Nearest-app-frame attribution of the malloc-family self samples (1,378 of
them), pre-change — this is the ranking the work is being done in:

| share of malloc samples | app parent |
|------------------------:|------------|
| 19.52% | `leaf_funnel::evaluate_leaf` |
| 11.18% | `leaf_funnel::tx_unit_inner` |
|  6.82% | `drop_glue::<Option<(Vec<i32>,Vec<i32>,u16,u16,Vec<u8>,Vec<u8>)>>` (`BlockDecision::chroma_dec`) |
|  6.60% | `drop_glue::<leaf_funnel::Cand>` |
|  6.31% | `<BlockDecision as Clone>::clone` |
|  6.10% | `partition::extract_neighbors_tiled` |
|  4.79% | `pd0::Pd0Ctx::lvl5_like_block_cost` |
|  3.56% | `pd0::tx_quant_core` |
|  3.27% | `drop_glue::<BlockDecision>` |

This contradicts the previously recorded claim that the traffic is so diffuse
that "the largest single parent is 2.4% of mem samples": at 1 ms self-time
resolution with nearest-app-ancestor attribution the top parent is 19.5% and
the top nine cover 68%. The 2.4% figure appears to have been computed over a
different (inclusive, per-call-site) grouping.
